<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bosnia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bosnia is a historical region of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, named after the river Bosna, which forms the larger, northern part of modern Bosnia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a banate and from 1377 a kingdom, home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnian-church">Bosnian Church</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> conquered it in 1463, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> occupied it in 1878 and annexed it in 1908.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bosnia.jpg" alt="Bosnia" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mladen Kolobarić · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bosnia_and_Herzegovina.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It entered the Partnership for Peace in 1994 and supported the IFOR and SFOR missions in <b>Bosnia</b>; as a NATO member from 1999 it was involved in the Yugoslav Wars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hungary">Hungary · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Louis I they campaigned widely: in Naples in 1347–1350 to avenge the murder of his brother Andrew, against Venice, which ceded Dalmatia in the Treaty of Zadar in 1358, and in the Balkans, where <b>Bosnia</b>, Serbian lords, Wallachia and Moldavia were drawn into Hungarian suzerainty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The agrarian reform begun in 1919 abolished the remaining serf-like tenancies in <b>Bosnia</b> and Macedonia and redistributed large estates, but productivity stayed low and rural overpopulation severe; in 1921 about half the adult population was illiterate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Malayan and Malaysian troops have served in United Nations missions from the Congo in the 1960s to Somalia and <b>Bosnia</b> in the 1990s and Lebanon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Within its neutrality Austria has a tradition of UN-led peacekeeping and humanitarian missions, with larger contingents in <b>Bosnia</b> and Kosovo and the all-volunteer Austrian Forces Disaster Relief Unit, able to deploy in ten hours.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-austria">Republic of Austria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Venice and the Ottoman authorities in <b>Bosnia</b> tried to take advantage of the disaster, but the republic survived, rebuilt in Baroque style and found new security in the Treaty of Karlowitz in 1699.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ragusa">Republic of Ragusa · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
